WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.340
Submit Puzzle Ideas helps you make your own
puzzle for The Fantastical Logic Grid,

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.226
step by step, and send it to the team.

3
00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:12.749
This video shows every entry, what it is for,

4
00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:16.467
and how it fits into the puzzle the wizard
builds.

5
00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:24.473
On thelogicgrid.com, choose Submit Puzzle
Ideas, under One account for every game.

6
00:00:25.523 --> 00:00:31.347
A box asks which puzzle you would like to
make. Choose The Fantastical Logic Grid.

7
00:00:32.397 --> 00:00:35.897
The wizard goes one step at a time. There are
6 steps,

8
00:00:35.917 --> 00:00:38.478
and you can jump back to any of them.

9
00:00:39.528 --> 00:00:43.309
Everything you type is saved on this device as
you go.

10
00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:47.209
Leave and come back later, and your draft is
waiting.

11
00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:50.397
Start over clears it when you want a fresh
start.

12
00:00:51.447 --> 00:00:56.397
Watch how it works opens this video and a
written guide to every entry.

13
00:00:56.747 --> 00:00:59.839
Show help on every field shows what to write,

14
00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:03.745
an example, and how each entry is used in your
puzzle.

15
00:01:04.795 --> 00:01:06.630
Step 1: Your story.

16
00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:11.973
Start with the world of your puzzle: its name,
the scene and how it ends.

17
00:01:13.023 --> 00:01:16.543
Title, which is required. The name of your
puzzle.

18
00:01:16.893 --> 00:01:19.827
Printed at the top of the puzzle and the PDF,

19
00:01:19.847 --> 00:01:23.017
and shown in Saved puzzles and in your credit.

20
00:01:24.067 --> 00:01:29.038
Look. The game theme whose colors, pictures
and music suit your story best.

21
00:01:29.388 --> 00:01:34.466
Picks the colors, scene pictures and music
when your puzzle is played in the game.

22
00:01:35.516 --> 00:01:40.437
Opening scene, which is required. Set the
scene in two to four sentences:

23
00:01:40.457 --> 00:01:44.669
where we are, and what went wrong or what
needs working out.

24
00:01:45.019 --> 00:01:47.799
Becomes the first paragraphs of the puzzle.

25
00:01:47.819 --> 00:01:52.657
The wizard adds who is in it, what to match
and the question at the end.

26
00:01:53.707 --> 00:01:56.791
Ending. What happens once the puzzle is
solved.

27
00:01:56.811 --> 00:02:00.726
Leave it empty and the wizard writes one from
the answer.

28
00:02:01.076 --> 00:02:05.237
Shown when the puzzle is solved, and printed
on the answer key.

29
00:02:06.287 --> 00:02:08.485
Tone. The feel of your story.

30
00:02:08.835 --> 00:02:14.425
Helps the team place your puzzle and choose
its music. It does not change the clues.

31
00:02:15.475 --> 00:02:19.658
Difficulty, which is required. How hard the
puzzle should be.

32
00:02:19.678 --> 00:02:22.515
Bigger grids are harder at every level.

33
00:02:22.865 --> 00:02:28.498
Sets which kinds of clues the wizard may use
and how much reasoning each step needs.

34
00:02:29.548 --> 00:02:31.298
Step 2: Who is in it.

35
00:02:31.648 --> 00:02:35.310
The people (or animals, robots…) your players
match up.

36
00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:38.091
They make the first column of the grid.

37
00:02:39.141 --> 00:02:41.243
Who they are, which is required.

38
00:02:41.263 --> 00:02:46.054
One word for the people in your story, and the
word for more than one.

39
00:02:46.404 --> 00:02:48.795
The heading of the grid's first column,

40
00:02:48.815 --> 00:02:52.613
and the word the clues and questions use
("Which guest…?").

41
00:02:53.663 --> 00:02:57.371
Names, which is required. 3 to 8 different
names.

42
00:02:57.391 --> 00:03:01.194
Every list you add needs one item for each
name.

43
00:03:01.544 --> 00:03:07.122
The first column of the grid. The number of
names sets the size of the grid:

44
00:03:07.142 --> 00:03:10.313
4 names make a 4 by 4 grid for each list.

45
00:03:11.363 --> 00:03:13.646
Step 3: What they are matched with.

46
00:03:13.996 --> 00:03:19.479
Two to four lists of things to match. Each
list becomes a column of the grid.

47
00:03:20.529 --> 00:03:25.996
Lists to match, which is required. Give each
list a name and its items,

48
00:03:26.016 --> 00:03:30.493
separated by commas. Use exactly one item for
each name.

49
00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:36.393
Each list becomes a column of the grid, and
the wizard writes clues about it.

50
00:03:36.413 --> 00:03:42.278
Ordered lists (times, prices, ages) also get
clues like "arrived earlier than".

51
00:03:43.328 --> 00:03:45.099
Step 4: The answer.

52
00:03:45.449 --> 00:03:46.865
Decide who has what.

53
00:03:46.885 --> 00:03:52.490
The wizard writes clues that lead to exactly
this answer, and only this one.

54
00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:59.215
Who decides the answer?. Choose the answer
yourself, or let the wizard pick one.

55
00:03:59.565 --> 00:04:03.257
The clues are written to lead to this answer
and no other,

56
00:04:03.277 --> 00:04:06.115
so every player reaches the same solution.

57
00:04:07.165 --> 00:04:11.528
Answer key. For each name, pick their item in
every list.

58
00:04:11.548 --> 00:04:14.313
Each item can go to one name only.

59
00:04:14.663 --> 00:04:18.265
Printed on the answer key page. The wizard
checks it,

60
00:04:18.285 --> 00:04:21.511
then writes clues that prove it step by step.

61
00:04:22.561 --> 00:04:24.909
Step 5: Pictures and sounds.

62
00:04:25.259 --> 00:04:29.206
Optional: add your own pictures and sounds for
your puzzle.

63
00:04:30.256 --> 00:04:35.436
Pictures and sounds. Up to 3 pictures and 2
sounds that you made yourself.

64
00:04:35.456 --> 00:04:41.158
For each one, say what it is for, whether we
may use it, and how to credit you.

65
00:04:41.508 --> 00:04:44.919
The team can use them with your puzzle in the
game:

66
00:04:44.939 --> 00:04:49.999
a cover or scene picture, a sound when it is
solved, or background music.

67
00:04:51.049 --> 00:04:55.467
At the bottom of every step, you see your
storyline as you write it:

68
00:04:55.487 --> 00:04:58.965
the puzzle's words, just as players will read
them.

69
00:04:59.315 --> 00:05:03.923
You cannot type in it. It changes as you fill
in the fields above.

70
00:05:04.973 --> 00:05:07.427
Step 6: Credit and permission.

71
00:05:07.777 --> 00:05:12.322
How you would like to be credited, and your
permission to use your puzzle.

72
00:05:13.372 --> 00:05:17.810
Author credit. The name shown in the author
credits of your puzzle.

73
00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.790
Printed on your puzzle and shown in the game's
credits if it is published.

74
00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:28.998
Permission, which is required. Tick the box to
say you made the puzzle yourself,

75
00:05:29.018 --> 00:05:33.505
and that the team may use and publish it with
the credit you chose.

76
00:05:33.855 --> 00:05:37.802
We can only save and publish your puzzle with
your permission.

77
00:05:38.852 --> 00:05:41.242
Check my puzzle opens a new page.

78
00:05:41.592 --> 00:05:44.994
The box at the top says whether your puzzle is
ready:

79
00:05:45.014 --> 00:05:49.038
it must have exactly one answer that players
can reason out.

80
00:05:49.388 --> 00:05:51.789
Anything missing or wrong is listed,

81
00:05:51.809 --> 00:05:56.599
with a link straight to the field, and
suggestions to make it better.

82
00:05:57.649 --> 00:06:02.749
Every entry is here in one editor, so you can
fix or change anything.

83
00:06:03.099 --> 00:06:05.754
Each change is checked again straight away,

84
00:06:05.774 --> 00:06:09.222
so you always know whether the puzzle is still
valid.

85
00:06:10.272 --> 00:06:13.174
When you are happy, press Build my puzzle.

86
00:06:13.524 --> 00:06:15.988
Download the PDF to keep or share it,

87
00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:20.522
or print it straight away. It has your puzzle
and its answer key.

88
00:06:20.872 --> 00:06:26.278
Your puzzle is also saved to your Saved
puzzles, so you can play it in the game,

89
00:06:26.298 --> 00:06:30.857
and a copy goes to the team, who may publish
it with your credit.

90
00:06:31.907 --> 00:06:36.334
Want to change something? Make changes takes
you back to the editor,

91
00:06:36.354 --> 00:06:41.017
with suggestions shown and the one-answer
check, then build it again.

92
00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:45.363
Answer the questions, check and fix, then
build,

93
00:06:45.383 --> 00:06:48.297
print and play. Have fun making puzzles!

